<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- Hardened-runtime entitlements. The runtime blocks microphone
     access by default, and the failure is a launch/first-use crash rather than
     a build error — so this file exists for exactly one key, and gains another
     only when a feature actually needs it.

     This file IS wired into src-tauri/tauri.conf.json (bundle.macOS
     "entitlements"), which is why it carries the microphone key and nothing
     else.

     keychain-access-groups deliberately absent; re-add only with an embedded
     provisioning profile. It is a restricted entitlement: claiming it from a
     WIRED file without an embedded Developer ID provisioning profile that
     authorizes it gets the app KILLED at launch by Trusted Execution
     (EXC_CRASH, Code Signature Invalid). The key sat here once while this file
     was unwired, so the claim never reached a signature; wiring the file for
     the microphone is what would make it real. To bring it back: create a
     Developer ID provisioning profile for this app's bundle identifier with
     Keychain Sharing, embed it as Contents/embedded.provisionprofile, THEN
     restore the key here. None of this shows up in a build or a test — the
     first honest check is a signed build, read back with codesign -d and its
     entitlements-print flag (two leading hyphens; a literal double hyphen
     here would make this comment invalid XML and break codesign itself). -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
